  h2: The conditions
  rows:
    - { title: Beta thalassaemia, body: "The body makes too little of the beta chain of haemoglobin. The severe form, thalassaemia major, needs blood transfusions for life. Carriers are usually healthy; their mild anaemia is often mistaken for iron deficiency. On average 3 to 4 in 100 Indians are carriers; when both parents are, each pregnancy has a 1 in 4 chance of thalassaemia major." }
    - { title: Alpha thalassaemia, body: "Changes in the alpha-globin genes, most often deletions. The effect runs from no symptoms to a lifelong anaemia, depending on how many of the four genes are affected. Routine screening often misses it." }
    - { title: Sickle-beta thalassaemia, body: "A person who inherits the sickle gene from one parent and a beta thalassaemia gene from the other. It can look like sickle cell disease, and it is common where both conditions run, as they do in the central belt. Telling the two apart matters for care and for counselling." }
    - { title: HbE and HbD, body: "Two other variants of haemoglobin. HbE is common in eastern and north-eastern India, and with a beta thalassaemia gene it can cause a serious anaemia. HbD, found in Punjab and Gujarat, can be mistaken for the sickle gene on a screening test." }
